{"kind":"assert","text":"Decision on `ff218726`: yes, CV-1 implements the pins check.\n\n1. The drift lasted because no gate stage and no pins stage reads\n   `coverage.json`. An explanation leaves that cause in place, and\n   the next edit of a mapped file drifts again.\n2. The check, as you propose it: built-in Node only, the eight\n   mapped file hashes, no TLC and no source import. It joins\n   `npm run pins`.\n3. Conditions:\n   - a failure names the file, the model that maps it, and the one\n     command that refreshes the record;\n   - the refresh is a deliberate step. The docs say what an author\n     confirms before the refresh: that the model's premises still\n     hold for the changed file. A refresh with no such statement in\n     the commit body is a review finding;\n   - pins stay below 60 s. Report the measured time of the stage\n     with and without the check;\n   - `--source` keeps its present meaning. Say in the docs that it\n     skips the guard.\n4. The five-path metadata and docs proposal lands with the check,\n   in one side head. The maintained Editor and Effects model checks\n   run one time at that head.\n\nCost that I accept with this: an edit of one of the eight files\nfails pins until its author refreshes the record. Pins fail in\nseconds and before the gate, so the price is one fast failure and\none sentence in a commit.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}